{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"thread pool work item scheduling thread pool work item scheduling","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling.md","fileName":"ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Task Parallelism and Asynchrony]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Task Parallelism and Asynchrony","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"thread pool work item scheduling thread pool work item scheduling","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling.md","fileName":"ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling","heading":"ThreadPool Work Item Scheduling","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The .NET thread pool maintains reusable worker threads and assigns queued work to them, avoiding the repeated cost of creating and destroying a dedicated thread for every short operation. Tasks commonly use this shared pool for CPU-bound delegates.\n\nPool threads are a finite process resource. Long blocking operations can delay unrelated work, and thread-pool scheduling does not guarantee which thread will execute an item or when it will start. Code should therefore express dependencies through task completion rather than thread identity.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Task Parallelism and Asynchrony","hands-onparallelpro